const ARTS=[{"s": "choose-your-next-smartphone", "g": "Smartphones", "k": 3, "m": 5, "t": "How to Choose Your Next Smartphone Without Overspending", "d": "A practical checklist for picking a phone that fits your needs and budget.", "p": ["Choosing a smartphone can feel confusing because every brand promises the best camera, the fastest chip and the longest battery. The easiest way to cut through the noise is to start with your own habits. Think about how you actually use your phone each day before you look at any price tag.", "Set a realistic budget first. Phones in every price range today handle calls, messaging, video and social apps well, so you rarely need the most expensive model. Decide what you are comfortable spending, then compare only the phones that fit inside that limit instead of stretching for extras you may never use.", "Pay attention to the display and battery, because you notice them every single day. A bright, smooth screen makes reading and watching video more pleasant, while a large battery with efficient charging saves you from carrying a charger everywhere. Reviews that test real battery life are more useful than the number printed on the box.", "Check how long the manufacturer promises software and security updates. A phone that keeps receiving updates stays safer and faster for more years, which makes it better value over time. Some brands now commit to several years of updates, and that promise can matter more than a small difference in processor speed.", "Be honest about storage and the camera. If you shoot many photos and videos, extra storage prevents the constant pain of deleting files. For cameras, look at sample photos taken in low light and on moving subjects, because that is where cheaper phones usually fall behind the better ones.", "If you can, hold the phone in a shop before buying. Comfort, weight and the feel of the buttons are personal, and a phone that looks perfect on paper may feel awkward in your hand. Finally, compare the price across a few trusted sellers and read the return policy before you pay."]}, {"s": "5g-explained", "g": "Technology", "k": 8, "m": 5, "t": "5G Explained: What It Really Means for Your Phone", "d": "How 5G differs from 4G, where it is faster and why coverage varies.", "p": ["5G is the fifth generation of mobile network technology, and it builds on the 4G networks most people use today. It promises faster data speeds, quicker response times and the ability to connect many more devices at once. Understanding what that means in daily life helps you judge whether it matters to you.", "The most visible benefit is speed. On a good 5G connection, large downloads and high-quality video streaming can finish much faster than on 4G. Real speeds, however, depend on your location, the network provider and how many people are using the same tower at the time.", "Another important improvement is latency, which is the delay between sending a request and getting a response. Lower latency makes video calls, online games and cloud applications feel more immediate. It also opens the door to uses such as remote control of machines and connected vehicles in the future.", "5G uses a range of radio frequencies. Lower bands travel far and pass through walls but offer moderate speeds, while higher bands deliver very high speeds over short distances and are easily blocked by buildings. Because of this, networks combine different bands and need many more small cells in busy areas.", "For this reason coverage varies widely. Some places have excellent 5G while others still depend mainly on 4G, and a phone icon showing 5G does not always mean top speed. To use the technology you need a 5G-capable phone, a suitable plan and coverage where you live and work.", "Should you upgrade? If your current phone and network already meet your needs, there is no rush. When you do buy your next phone, choosing one with 5G support is sensible, because networks will keep expanding and the phone will stay useful for longer."]}, {"s": "what-is-artificial-intelligence", "g": "AI", "k": 9, "m": 5, "t": "What Is Artificial Intelligence? A Simple Guide", "d": "A clear introduction to AI, how it learns from data and where you already use it.", "p": ["Artificial intelligence, usually shortened to AI, is the ability of a computer system to perform tasks that normally need human intelligence. These tasks include recognising speech, understanding images, translating languages and making recommendations. It is not one single technology, but a broad field with many methods.", "Most modern AI relies on machine learning. Instead of programmers writing exact rules for every situation, the system is shown a very large number of examples and learns patterns from them. For instance, a program shown millions of labelled pictures can learn to tell cats from dogs.", "You probably use AI every day without noticing. Your phone suggests the next word while you type, maps estimate how long a journey will take, streaming services recommend what to watch and email filters move spam out of your inbox. These features are based on patterns found in huge amounts of data.", "AI is useful because it can process far more information than a person, and it can do so quickly and without getting tired. In medicine it can help examine scans, in farming it can help monitor crops, and in schools it can help adapt practice questions to a student's level.", "It also has limits. AI systems can make mistakes, and they can reflect biases that exist in the data they learned from. They do not truly understand the world as people do, which is why important decisions, especially those affecting health or fairness, still need human judgement and oversight.", "The best way to approach AI is with curiosity and care. Learn what it can do, check important results yourself, and be thoughtful about the personal information you share with any service. Knowing the basics makes it easier to use these tools wisely instead of fearing or trusting them blindly."]}, {"s": "how-rockets-escape-gravity", "g": "Science", "k": 6, "m": 5, "t": "How Does a Rocket Escape Earth's Gravity?", "d": "The physics of launching a rocket, from thrust to orbit.", "p": ["Earth's gravity pulls everything towards the ground, so leaving the planet takes a huge amount of energy. A rocket does not simply fly upward until gravity stops. It must build enough speed and keep pushing against gravity for several minutes while burning large amounts of fuel.", "A rocket works because of Newton's third law of motion: every action has an equal and opposite reaction. The engine throws hot gas downward at high speed, and the gas pushes the rocket upward with an equal force. This works even in the vacuum of space, because the rocket pushes against its own exhaust, not against the air.", "To escape Earth completely and never fall back, an object needs a speed of about eleven kilometres per second, known as escape velocity. Most satellites do not need that much. They stay in orbit around the Earth at roughly eight kilometres per second, high enough to be above most of the atmosphere.", "Orbit itself is a kind of constant falling. A satellite moves sideways so fast that as it falls towards Earth, the curved surface drops away beneath it. It keeps falling around the planet without ever hitting the ground, which is why astronauts on the space station float.", "Rockets are built in stages because fuel is heavy. Once the first stage has used its fuel, it is dropped to remove dead weight, and the next stage continues to push the smaller remaining rocket higher and faster. Engineers also shape the path so that the rocket climbs through the thickest air while it is not yet moving too fast.", "In recent years some rockets have been designed so their first stages can land and fly again. Reusing expensive parts lowers the cost of reaching space, which makes it easier to launch satellites for communication, weather and navigation, and to explore farther into the solar system."]}, {"s": "what-is-a-link-shortener", "g": "Basics", "m": 4, "t": "What Is a Link Shortener and How Does It Work?", "d": "A plain-language guide to how short links are created, stored and opened.", "p": ["A link shortener turns a long, messy web address into a short one that is easy to share. Instead of pasting a link that wraps across three lines in a chat, you share something compact that looks clean and is simple to remember.", "Behind the scenes, the idea is simple. When you create a short link, the service stores your original address in a database next to a short code. When someone opens the short link, the service looks up that code and sends the visitor to the original page.", "This extra step is also what makes shorteners useful. Because every visit passes through the service first, it can count clicks, record the country a visitor comes from, and show you which platforms send the most traffic.", "Many creators use short links in bios, captions, newsletters and chat groups, where long addresses look untidy or get cut off. A custom alias, such as a word that describes the content, also makes a link easier to trust and remember.", "Some shorteners show a short page before the final destination. That page can carry advertising, which is how creators earn from the traffic they send. The best experience keeps this page fast, clear and honest about what happens next.", "When choosing a shortener, look at reliability, clear analytics, how it protects visitors from harmful links, and whether payouts and rules are explained plainly. A good service is transparent about all four."], "k": 0}, {"s": "grow-your-audience", "g": "Growth", "m": 4, "t": "5 Simple Ways to Grow Your Audience Before Sharing Links", "d": "Practical habits that bring real, interested visitors to the links you share.", "p": ["Earnings from shared links depend on one thing above all: real people who are interested in what you post. Tools can measure and manage traffic, but they cannot create an audience for you. Building that audience is the most valuable work a creator does.", "Pick a clear niche. Accounts that focus on one topic, such as study tips, gaming guides or recipes, are easier for new people to understand and follow. When someone sees your page, they should know within seconds what they will get from you.", "Post consistently. A steady schedule, even three times a week, builds trust better than bursts of activity followed by silence. Followers remember creators who show up regularly and deliver useful content each time.", "Share value first and links second. Explain what the link contains, why it helps, and what the reader will find. People click when they understand the benefit, and they come back when the content matches what you promised.", "Take part in communities. Answer questions in groups, reply to comments and thank people who share your work. Real conversations create loyal followers, and loyal followers are the most reliable source of repeat visits.", "Finally, review your analytics. Check which posts and platforms bring the most visitors, then do more of what works. Small improvements repeated over time matter far more than any single viral moment."], "k": 8}, {"s": "spot-unsafe-links", "g": "Safety", "m": 4, "t": "How to Spot Unsafe Links Before You Click", "d": "Simple habits that protect you from scams hidden behind short links.", "p": ["Short links hide the real destination, which is convenient but also a risk. Scammers sometimes use them to disguise fake login pages and harmful downloads. A few simple habits can protect you almost every time.", "Think about where the link came from. A message from an unknown number or an unexpected email deserves extra care, even if it looks friendly. If a friend sends something strange, check with them through another channel before opening it.", "Watch for pressure. Messages that say you must act right now, claim you have won a prize or warn that your account will be closed are common tricks. Real organisations rarely demand instant action through a random link.", "Look closely at the address after the page opens. Misspelled brand names, extra words or strange endings are warning signs. A padlock icon only shows that the connection is encrypted, not that the website is trustworthy.", "Never enter passwords, card numbers or one-time codes on a page you reached through an unverified link. If you need to log in to a service, type its address yourself or use its official app instead.", "Keep your browser and phone updated, and use built-in safe browsing warnings. If you do open a suspicious page by mistake, close it, avoid entering anything, and change any password you may have shared."], "k": 2}, {"s": "understanding-cpm", "g": "Earnings", "m": 5, "t": "Understanding CPM: Why Some Clicks Earn More Than Others", "d": "What CPM means and why the same traffic can earn different amounts.", "p": ["CPM stands for cost per mille, which means the amount advertisers pay for one thousand ad views. It is the most common way to describe how much a publisher earns from advertising, and it explains why two sites with the same traffic can earn very different amounts.", "Location matters a great deal. Advertisers often pay more to reach audiences in countries where their products are widely sold, so the same number of views can produce different earnings depending on where visitors live.", "Device and format also play a role. Video ads usually pay more than small banners, and ads that people actually see on screen tend to be worth more than ads that load out of view. Page speed and a clean layout help ads display properly.", "Timing changes the numbers too. Advertising budgets are often higher around major holidays and shopping seasons, so rates can rise and fall during the year. Because of this, a single day's result is rarely a reliable guide.", "No one can promise a fixed rate. Real CPM depends on advertisers, audience and quality of traffic, and genuine visitors matter most. Clicks generated by bots or repeated visits are filtered out and can lead to account problems.", "The best long-term approach is to focus on visitors who are really interested in your content. Steady, genuine traffic is what keeps advertisers paying and keeps your earnings stable."], "k": 1}, {"s": "smartphone-cameras-explained", "g": "Smartphones", "k": 3, "m": 5, "t": "Smartphone Cameras Explained: Why Megapixels Are Not Everything", "d": "What really decides photo quality on a phone, from sensors to software.", "p": ["Phone makers love to advertise megapixels, and a bigger number sounds better. In reality, the megapixel count only tells you how many tiny dots make up the image. It says very little about how good those dots look, especially when the light is poor.", "The sensor matters far more. A physically larger sensor can collect more light, which gives cleaner photos at night and better detail in shadows. When two phones have the same megapixels, the one with the larger sensor and larger individual pixels usually produces the more natural result.", "The lens is the second key part. A wider aperture lets in more light, and good glass keeps edges of the picture sharp. Optical image stabilisation helps too, because it steadies the camera during longer exposures so that night shots and videos come out less blurry.", "Modern phones also depend heavily on software. When you press the shutter, the phone often combines several frames, balances bright and dark areas and reduces noise in a fraction of a second. This computational photography is why a small camera can now produce pictures that once needed larger equipment.", "Be careful with zoom claims. Optical zoom uses a real lens that moves or a dedicated telephoto camera, so the detail is genuine. Digital zoom simply crops and enlarges the picture, which loses detail. A phone with a true telephoto lens will almost always beat one that relies on digital zoom.", "To get better photos from any phone, wipe the lens, tap the screen to set focus, and look for soft natural light. A clean lens and good lighting improve a picture more than any specification on the box, so learn those habits before spending money on a new phone."]}, {"s": "how-wifi-works-stronger-signal", "g": "Technology", "k": 8, "m": 5, "t": "How Wi-Fi Works and How to Get a Stronger Signal at Home", "d": "A simple explanation of Wi-Fi and practical ways to fix a weak signal.", "p": ["Wi-Fi lets your devices connect to the internet without cables. A router receives the internet connection from your provider and turns it into radio signals that phones, laptops and televisions can pick up. Those signals travel through the air much like radio, but on frequencies designed for short distances.", "Most routers use two main bands. The 2.4 GHz band reaches farther and passes through walls more easily, but it is slower and often crowded. The 5 GHz band is faster and less congested, but its range is shorter. Many modern routers also support newer standards that improve speed and handle many devices better.", "Placement is the easiest fix for a weak signal. Put the router in a central, open and slightly raised position instead of inside a cupboard or at the far corner of the house. Thick walls, metal objects, mirrors and large appliances can all block or weaken the signal.", "Interference also matters. Microwave ovens, cordless phones and neighbouring networks can crowd the same channels. Most routers can choose a less busy channel automatically, and you can change it in the settings if the connection keeps dropping at certain times of day.", "If one router cannot cover the whole home, consider a mesh system or a wired access point. These spread the signal across several locations and are usually more reliable than cheap range extenders. For devices that stay in one place, such as a desktop or television, a network cable gives the most stable connection.", "Finally, keep your router secure and updated. Use a strong password, choose modern encryption such as WPA2 or WPA3, and install firmware updates when they are available. Restarting the router occasionally can also clear small glitches and restore normal speed."]}, {"s": "how-chatbots-learn-to-write", "g": "AI", "k": 9, "m": 5, "t": "How AI Chatbots Learn to Write: A Plain-Language Look", "d": "What happens behind the scenes when a chatbot answers your question.", "p": ["AI chatbots can answer questions, summarise articles and draft emails in a conversational style. It can seem almost magical, but the basic idea behind them is easier to understand than it looks. They are computer programs that have learned patterns in language from a very large collection of text.", "During training, a model reads enormous amounts of writing and practises predicting what word or piece of a word is likely to come next in a sentence. By repeating this process billions of times, it learns grammar, common facts, styles of writing and how ideas are usually connected.", "After this first stage, developers often refine the model further. People review answers and rate which ones are more helpful, clear and safe, and the system is adjusted to prefer those kinds of responses. This extra training helps the chatbot follow instructions and respond politely.", "When you ask a question, the chatbot does not look up one stored answer. It produces a response piece by piece, choosing each next word based on your message and the patterns it learned. That is why the same question can sometimes receive slightly different answers.", "This method has an important weakness. A chatbot can sound confident while being wrong, because it is built to produce fluent text, not to guarantee truth. It can also mix up details or invent sources, so facts, numbers and quotations should always be checked against reliable references.", "Used well, chatbots are excellent helpers for brainstorming, explaining difficult ideas and improving your writing. Give clear instructions, review what they produce, and avoid sharing private details such as passwords or financial information. Treat the answer as a first draft that you are responsible for checking."]}, {"s": "why-is-the-sky-blue", "g": "Science", "k": 5, "m": 4, "t": "Why Is the Sky Blue? The Science of Scattered Light", "d": "How sunlight, air and scattering create the colours of the sky.", "p": ["On a clear day the sky looks blue, and at sunset it turns orange and red. The answer to why this happens lies in the way sunlight interacts with the air around us. It is a lovely example of simple physics shaping something we see every day.", "Sunlight looks white, but it is actually a mixture of all the colours of the rainbow. Each colour has a different wavelength, with red light having long waves and blue and violet light having much shorter ones. When sunlight enters the atmosphere, it meets countless tiny gas molecules.", "These molecules scatter the light in all directions, and shorter wavelengths are scattered much more strongly than longer ones. This effect is called Rayleigh scattering. Blue light is therefore bounced all around the sky, so wherever you look you see some of it coming towards your eyes.", "You might wonder why the sky is not violet, since violet has an even shorter wavelength. Part of the answer is that the Sun gives off less violet light, some is absorbed high in the atmosphere, and our eyes are less sensitive to violet than to blue. The mixture that reaches us looks blue.", "At sunrise and sunset the Sun is low, and its light must pass through much more air to reach you. Most of the blue light is scattered away along the way, leaving mainly the longer wavelengths. That is why the sky near the horizon glows with orange and red.", "The same idea explains why distant mountains look bluish and why the sky looks darker at high altitude, where there is less air to scatter light. Next time you look up, remember that you are seeing sunlight that has been scattered by the atmosphere itself."]}, {"s": "make-phone-battery-last-longer", "g": "Smartphones", "k": 7, "m": 4, "t": "How to Make Your Phone Battery Last Longer", "d": "Everyday habits that reduce battery wear and stretch the time between charges.", "p": ["Every phone battery slowly loses capacity as it ages, and that is normal. The good news is that a few simple habits can slow the wear and also help you get through each day with more charge left. None of them require special apps or technical skill.", "Screen brightness is one of the biggest drains. Lowering brightness or using automatic brightness saves a noticeable amount of power. On phones with OLED screens, dark mode can help as well, because black pixels use very little energy on those displays.", "Heat is the enemy of batteries. Avoid leaving your phone in a hot car, under a pillow while charging or in direct sun for long periods. If the phone feels hot while you play a demanding game or record video, give it a break so it can cool down.", "Many battery experts suggest avoiding the extremes. Keeping the charge between roughly twenty and eighty percent most of the time is gentler on lithium-ion cells than draining to zero or sitting at one hundred for days. Occasional full charges are fine, so there is no need to be strict.", "Check which apps use the most power in your battery settings. Apps that refresh in the background, track location constantly or send many notifications can quietly drain a lot of charge. Restrict background activity for apps you rarely use, and turn off features such as constant location access if you do not need them.", "Use power saving mode when you are running low, keep your software updated because updates often improve efficiency, and use the charger that came with your phone or one from a trusted brand. If battery health drops a lot, a replacement from an authorised service centre is often cheaper than a new phone."]}, {"s": "what-is-cloud-storage", "g": "Technology", "k": 2, "m": 4, "t": "What Is Cloud Storage and Is It Safe to Use?", "d": "How cloud storage works, its benefits and how to keep your files protected.", "p": ["Cloud storage means keeping your files on powerful computers owned by a service provider and reached through the internet, instead of only on your phone or laptop. The word cloud is just a friendly way to say your data lives on remote servers that you can access from almost any device.", "The biggest advantage is convenience. Photos, documents and backups are available wherever you sign in, and sharing a file becomes as simple as sending a link. If your phone is lost or damaged, the files you saved in the cloud are still safe and can be restored on a new device.", "Cloud services are usually well protected. Reputable providers store your data in secure data centres, make several copies and use encryption while files travel and while they are stored. This is often safer than keeping the only copy of important files on a single device that can break or be stolen.", "The weakest point is usually the account, not the servers. If someone learns your password, they can open your files. Use a long unique password and turn on two-factor authentication, so that a stolen password alone is not enough to get in.", "Think carefully about what you upload. Very sensitive files, such as copies of identity documents, can be encrypted before uploading or kept offline. Also read the privacy settings of the service, and avoid sharing links publicly unless you really want anyone with the link to see the file.", "A good approach is to follow the habit of keeping more than one copy of important data: one on your device, one in the cloud and, for the most important files, one on an external drive. That way a single failure never means losing everything."]}, {"s": "what-are-black-holes", "g": "Science", "k": 6, "m": 5, "t": "What Are Black Holes? A Beginner's Guide", "d": "How black holes form, why light cannot escape and what scientists have observed.", "p": ["A black hole is a region of space where gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape once it gets too close. The idea sounds like science fiction, but black holes are a real prediction of Einstein's theory of gravity and astronomers have found strong evidence for them.", "Many black holes form when a very massive star reaches the end of its life. After it runs out of fuel, the star can no longer hold itself up against its own gravity, and its core collapses into an extremely small and dense object. The result is a stellar black hole.", "The boundary around a black hole is called the event horizon. It is not a solid surface but a point of no return. Anything that crosses it, whether a particle, a star or a beam of light, cannot get back out because it would need to travel faster than light.", "Black holes are invisible by themselves because they give off no light, so scientists find them by their effects. Gas falling towards a black hole becomes extremely hot and shines brightly, and stars near one move in ways that reveal an unseen heavy object. In 2019 astronomers also released the first image of a black hole's shadow.", "There are also much larger supermassive black holes, which sit at the centres of most large galaxies, including our own Milky Way. Their masses can equal millions or even billions of suns. How they grew so large is still a major topic of research.", "A common worry is that black holes might swallow everything around them, but they behave like any other object with the same mass. If our Sun were replaced by a black hole of equal mass, the planets would continue to orbit as before, although it would be very dark and cold."]}, {"s": "why-your-phone-gets-slow", "g": "Smartphones", "k": 4, "m": 5, "t": "Why Your Phone Gets Slow and How to Fix It", "d": "Common reasons phones lose speed over time and the steps that really help.", "p": ["It is frustrating when a phone that once felt quick starts to lag, freeze or take ages to open apps. The cause is rarely one single problem. Usually several small things add up over months of use, and most of them can be fixed without buying anything.", "The most common culprit is nearly full storage. Phones need free space to move data around, and when storage is almost full everything slows down. Delete unused apps, clear old downloads and move photos and videos to cloud storage or a computer to free up room.", "Too many apps running in the background can also slow things down. Each one uses memory and processing power, even when you are not looking at it. Closing apps you do not need, and limiting background activity in settings, gives your phone more room to work.", "Old software can be a problem too. Updates often include performance improvements and fixes for bugs that cause slowness, so installing them is usually worthwhile. On the other hand, very old phones may struggle with the newest versions, so check reviews from other owners before updating.", "Aging batteries and heat can reduce speed as well. Some phones deliberately slow down to prevent shutdowns when the battery is worn, and overheating makes the processor reduce its speed to protect itself. Replacing a worn battery can bring back a surprising amount of smoothness.", "Start with the simple steps: restart the phone, free some storage, remove apps you do not use and update your software. If it is still slow, back up your data and try a factory reset as a last step, because that clears accumulated clutter and often makes an old phone feel fresh again."]}, {"s": "how-search-engines-work", "g": "Technology", "k": 0, "m": 5, "t": "How Search Engines Find and Rank Web Pages", "d": "The three steps behind every search result: crawling, indexing and ranking.", "p": ["When you type a question into a search engine, results appear in a fraction of a second. That speed hides a huge amount of work that happened long before you pressed the button. Understanding it helps both readers and anyone who publishes content online.", "The first step is crawling. Search engines use automated programs, often called crawlers or bots, that travel across the web by following links from one page to another. They read each page they find and record what it contains, which is how new pages get discovered.", "The second step is indexing. The information collected by crawlers is organised into an enormous index, similar to the index at the back of a book. Instead of searching the whole web when you ask a question, the engine searches this prepared index, which is why results come back so quickly.", "The third step is ranking. The engine decides which pages best answer your search and puts them first. It considers many signals, such as how closely the content matches the question, how useful and trustworthy the page appears, how fast it loads and whether it works well on a phone.", "Links from other reputable sites still help, because they act like recommendations. However, quality matters more than quantity. A few links from relevant, trusted pages are worth much more than many from low-quality sources, and attempts to trick the system tend to be caught over time.", "For creators, the lesson is simple. Write clear, original content that really answers what people are looking for, make your pages fast and easy to read on mobile, and organise your site with sensible titles and links. There is no secret shortcut that replaces being genuinely helpful."]}, {"s": "how-solar-panels-work", "g": "Science", "k": 7, "m": 4, "t": "How Do Solar Panels Turn Sunlight into Electricity?", "d": "The photovoltaic effect explained, with practical notes on efficiency.", "p": ["Solar panels convert sunlight directly into electricity, with no moving parts and no fuel to burn. They are used on rooftops, in large solar farms, on calculators and on satellites. The key idea behind them is a process called the photovoltaic effect.", "Most panels are made from silicon, a material that is a semiconductor. A solar cell has two layers of silicon treated differently so that together they create an electric field. When particles of light, called photons, strike the cell, they knock electrons loose from the silicon atoms.", "The electric field pushes these free electrons in one direction, which creates a flow of electric current. Metal contacts on the cell collect this current. At this stage it is direct current, the same type of electricity that batteries produce.", "Homes and the power grid use alternating current, so an inverter changes the electricity into the right form. Many systems also include a battery that stores extra energy during the day so it can be used in the evening, or they send surplus power back to the grid.", "The efficiency of a panel tells how much of the sunlight is converted into electricity. Good modern panels convert roughly one fifth of the sunlight that hits them. Output depends on the angle of the panel, shade, temperature and the weather, although panels still make some power on cloudy days.", "Solar energy produces no direct air pollution while generating power, and the cost of panels has fallen steadily over the past years. Panels do need space, careful installation and occasional cleaning, and the energy used to make them is paid back after some time of operation."]}, {"s": "processors-cores-and-speed-explained", "g": "Technology", "k": 4, "m": 5, "t": "What Is a Processor? Cores, Speed and Performance Explained", "d": "What the chip in your phone or laptop actually does and how to read the specs.", "p": ["The processor is the brain of a phone or computer. It carries out the instructions that apps and the operating system send to it, from opening a web page to editing a photo. When people say a device is fast, a large part of that speed comes from its processor.", "A processor contains several cores, and each core can work on its own set of tasks. More cores let the device handle many jobs at the same time, such as playing music while you browse and download a file. Many phone chips mix powerful cores for heavy work with efficient cores for light work to save battery.", "Clock speed, measured in gigahertz, shows how many cycles a core can perform each second. A higher number can mean faster work, but it is not the full story. Two chips with the same clock speed can perform very differently because of how efficiently their designs use each cycle.", "Other parts matter too. Cache is a small, extremely fast memory that stores data the processor needs again and again. Memory, called RAM, holds the apps you are currently using, and a graphics unit handles images and games. All these parts work together, so a balanced device usually feels better than one with a single strong spec.", "Heat and power limits also shape performance. Slim phones cannot remove heat as easily as large laptops, so a chip may reduce its speed after long heavy use. That is why benchmark scores for a short test can look much better than the performance you feel during a long gaming session.", "When comparing devices, read independent reviews that test real tasks, such as app launching, video editing and gaming. Choose the level of performance you actually need. A mid-range processor handles everyday use very well, and paying extra only makes sense if you do demanding work."]}, {"s": "atoms-and-molecules-explained", "g": "Science", "k": 5, "m": 4, "t": "Atoms and Molecules: The Building Blocks of Everything", "d": "A simple tour of atoms, elements and molecules and how they combine.", "p": ["Everything around you, from the air you breathe to the phone in your hand, is made of tiny particles called atoms. They are so small that millions of them could fit across the width of a single human hair. Understanding atoms is the first step to understanding chemistry and much of physics.", "An atom has a central nucleus containing protons and neutrons, with much lighter electrons moving around it. Protons carry a positive electric charge, electrons carry a negative charge, and neutrons carry no charge. In a neutral atom the number of protons equals the number of electrons.", "The number of protons decides which element an atom belongs to. An atom with one proton is hydrogen, one with six is carbon and one with eight is oxygen. The periodic table arranges all the known elements in order, and it groups together elements that behave in similar ways.", "Atoms often join together to form molecules by sharing or transferring electrons, creating chemical bonds. Water is a familiar example: each molecule contains two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, which is why it is written as H2O. Different combinations of the same few elements make a huge variety of substances.", "Although we picture atoms as tiny solid balls, most of an atom is empty space. The nucleus is incredibly small compared with the whole atom, and the electrons exist in regions described by quantum physics rather than neat circular paths. Scientists use models to make these ideas easier to picture.", "These ideas have practical value. The study of atoms and molecules led to modern medicines, materials such as plastics and the semiconductors in every smartphone. Even a basic understanding helps explain why matter behaves as it does, from boiling water to rusting iron."]}];
